  "narrative": "On October 22, 2026, the Karnataka High Court recorded an overall hearing rate of 96.7%, hearing 89 of the 92 scheduled matters across three active benches. This represents a substantial rise in hearing efficiency compared to previous sessions, which logged overall rates of 52.3% on October 17 (955 of 1,827 heard), 40.1% on October 16 (1,221 of 3,046 heard), and 38.8% on October 15 (1,247 of 3,217 heard), driven by a significantly lighter scheduled docket.\n\nBench throughput remained consistently high across all three court halls. In Hall 11, Justice Raghavendra Seetharam Srivatsa achieved a 100% clearance rate, hearing all 32 listed matters. Justice G Basavaraja handled the largest single-judge docket in Hall 10, hearing 47 of 48 scheduled cases (97.9%). The division bench of Justices R. Nataraj and B. Pramod in Hall 9 heard 10 of their 12 scheduled matters (83.3%).\n\nAll formal procedural stages registered complete hearing conversion: Orders (21 scheduled), Admission (16 scheduled), and Preliminary Hearing (10 scheduled) each achieved a 100% hearing rate. This marks a sharp contrast to October 17, when Orders reached 56.1%, Admission reached 47.4%, and Preliminary Hearing reached 51.6%. Matters with unspecified procedural stages saw 42 of 45 cases heard (93.3%).\n\nThe day’s docket was heavily concentrated in Criminal Petitions (CRL.P) and Writ Petitions (WP), which together constituted 82.6% of all scheduled listings. Writ Petitions attained a 100% hearing rate across 37 cases, improving notably over historical clearance rates that ranged between 34.3% and 48.7% between October 15 and 17. Criminal Petitions saw 38 of 39 matters heard (97.4%), while Writ Appeals recorded the lowest clearance among multi-case categories, with 2 of 4 matters heard (50.0%).",
